# Solutions

**1.** (p−k)²−m²=p²−2p·k+k²−m²=−2p·k+k² on shell.

**2.** k²≪2p·k for a soft photon.

**3.** ū γ·ε (p̸+m) ~ 2p·ε ū, using p̸u=mu.

**4.** Vertex e, numerator 2p·ε, denominator −2p·k, overall e(p·ε)/(p·k) up to a conventional sign from (p−k) versus (p+k).

**5.** Outgoing emission uses p·ε/p·k with a relative minus compared with absorption on an incoming line, equivalent to assigning −e to the opposite charge flow.

**6.** Each leg contributes e_i; Σ e_i=0.

**7.** In a suitable frame Σ_pol |p·ε|² ~ p_perp², leaving 1/(p·k)² ~ 1/ω².

**8.** Photon measure ω² dω dΩ / ω combined with 1/ω² gives dω/ω dΩ.

**9.** Infrared cutoff to detector resolution.

**10.** The smallest photon energy the experiment can see.

**11.** −c(α/π)log(E/λ) times |ℳ_tree|².

**12.** c(α/π)log(ΔE/λ)−c(α/π)log(E/λ)=c(α/π)log(ΔE/E).

**13.** Yes, to guarantee the virtual and real coefficients match and that ℳ_soft is gauge invariant.

**14.** A real photon collinear with the outgoing electron, not a soft photon in an unrelated direction.

**15.** The propagator approximation (p−k)²−m²≈−2p·k fails when k is not small.

**16.** Each extra soft photon multiplies by another eikonal factor; the sum over n photons exponentiates the logarithm, the Sudakov factor.

